.TH ID 1 2026-05-11 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
id \- print real and effective user and group IDs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B id
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.RI [ USER ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR id
utility prints real and effective user and group IDs for each
.IR USER ,
or for the current process when no
.I USER
operand is given.
.PP
With no options, output follows the POSIX format
.RS
uid=\fIN\fR(\fIname\fR) gid=\fIN\fR(\fIname\fR) [euid=...] [egid=...] groups=...
.RE
where
.B euid
and
.B egid
are emitted only when they differ from the corresponding real IDs.
Substrate does not append a
.BR context=
field even when SELinux-style labels would be available; this matches
BSD behaviour.  The per-flag conflict-resolution policy between
POSIX, BSD, and GNU is documented in
.BR docs/id.md
in the source tree.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output mode (mutually exclusive)
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user
Print only the effective user ID.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group
Print only the effective group ID.
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\-groups
Print all group IDs (effective, real, supplementary) in that order,
deduplicated.
.TP
.B \-p
BSD: print a human-readable summary.
.TP
.B \-P
BSD: print a
.BR passwd (5)-style
record for the user.
.SS Modifiers
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name
Print the name instead of the numeric ID.  Only valid with
.BR \-u ", " \-g ", or " \-G .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-real
Print the real ID instead of the effective ID.  Only valid with
.BR \-u " or " \-g .
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
Delimit output with NUL bytes rather than whitespace.  Not permitted
in the default format.  With multiple
.I USER
operands and
.BR \-G ,
each user's group list ends with an additional NUL so records are
self-delimited.
.TP
.B \-a
Accepted for compatibility with other implementations; ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display a short usage message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SS Unsupported on this platform
The flags
.BR \-A " (audit user ID), " \-M " (MAC label), " \-R " (login class or"
.RB "routing table, depending on BSD), " \-Z " (security context), and"
.RB "BSD " \-c " / " \-d " / " \-s
are recognised but always report
.RS
.B id: option unsupported on this platform
.RE
because Substrate does not carry the corresponding subsystems.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requested information was printed.
.TP
.B 1
A
.I USER
operand did not resolve, an unsupported mode was requested, or option
parsing failed.  With multiple
.I USER
operands, exit status is the OR of per-user results: processing
continues even after a per-user failure.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B "'USER': no such user"
.BR getpwnam (3)
returned NULL for the named user.
.TP
.B "cannot print multiple formats"
More than one of
.BR \-u ", " \-g ", " \-G ", " \-p ", " \-P
was given.
.TP
.B "printing only names or real IDs requires -u, -g, or -G"
.B \-n
or
.B \-r
was given without an accompanying mode flag.
.TP
.B "option --zero not permitted in default format"
.B \-z
was given without a mode flag.  The default
.BR uid=...
format is not NUL-delimitable.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the current user's full record:
.EX
$ id
uid=1000(segin) gid=998(wheel) groups=998(wheel),50(games),1000(flutter)
.EE
.PP
Numeric effective UID only:
.EX
$ id -u
1000
.EE
.PP
Names of all groups, NUL-delimited:
.EX
$ id -Gnz | xargs -0 -n1
wheel
games
flutter
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR whoami (1),
.BR groups (1),
.BR getuid (2),
.BR getgid (2),
.BR getgroups (2),
.BR getpwnam (3),
.BR getgrgid (3),
.BR passwd (5),
.BR group (5)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.BR \-u ", " \-g ", " \-G ", " \-n ", and " \-r
options conform to POSIX.1-2024.  The
.BR \-p " and " \-P
options follow FreeBSD convention.  The
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero ", " \-\-help ", and " \-\-version
options follow GNU coreutils.  Where the three traditions disagree,
Substrate follows POSIX first, then BSD, then GNU.
.SH HISTORY
.BR id
first appeared in System III.  The BSD
.BR \-p " and " \-P
variants are from 4.4BSD.  GNU added
.BR \-Z " and " \-\-zero
in coreutils 8.x.
